module param_mode.pos_1

// A parameter MODE, and the effect is VISIBLE: the program prints 42, byte for
// byte with the oracle. `mut v int` is what std writes 131 times.
//
// The argument is a `mut` BINDING on purpose. A mode is an axis of overloading
// (P14) and the rule filters per position: a `mut` parameter needs a mutable
// place, so `ro x = 41; bump(x)` fits nothing and novac refuses it. The oracle
// is LAXER there for a VALUE type -- it accepts the `ro` binding, because for
// `int` a `mut` parameter is the callee's own copy rather than an in-out. That
// corner is the spec's own unfinished pass
// (`[M-184-value-mut-mode-overload-abi]`), and for a HEAP type the oracle
// refuses the same shape outright (E_READONLY_COERCE, measured 2026-08-21):
// novac keeps the rule, and the divergence is registered.
//
// novac REFUSED this file entirely until 2026-08-21, and the refusal wore a lie
// for a face: "novac does not know this type yet", pointing at `int`. Both
// collectors read a parameter's type by the fixed index `pk[1]`, which is the
// MODE's slot when a mode is written -- so the NAME was read as the type.
fn bump(mut v int) -> int => v + 1

fn main() {
    mut m = 41
    println(bump(m))
}
